1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,120
In little more than a century, we've gone from dirt tracks to superhighways.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:12,500
From the first internal combustion engines to vehicles that tell us how, where and when to drive.

3
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:19,200
We have filled our roadways and our airways and sea lanes almost to capacity

4
00:00:19,201 --> 00:00:22,040
and nearly doubled our commute time in traffic.

5
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:26,320
Traffic that must be controlled, managed and regulated.

6
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:30,400
Now, Trapping on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:47,940
In 1939, this Firebird was a prototype for the car of the future.

8
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:52,740
A sleek, rocket-like automated vehicle that could virtually drive itself.

9
00:00:52,741 --> 00:00:59,740
Today, we still can't cross the country in 24 hours, as envisioned by some of the Firebird's designers.

10
00:00:59,741 --> 00:01:07,740
But the new millennium does promise to relieve us of much of the tedium and frustration of operating a vehicle in heavy congestion,

11
00:01:07,741 --> 00:01:13,740
thanks to the advent of Intelligent Transportation Systems, or ITS.

12
00:01:13,741 --> 00:01:19,740
Intelligent transportation systems apply the technology of the information age,

13
00:01:19,741 --> 00:01:24,720
communication, computers, sensors, to solving transportation problems.

14
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:31,660
We see them on television in the navigation system the rental cars are offering.

15
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:39,260
We will see them in our highway systems as the equivalent of air traffic control,

16
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:46,100
except it will be ground traffic control that helps us manage the traffic system that we use every day.

17
00:01:46,101 --> 00:01:53,040
One element of ITS that we may soon see on our vehicles is adaptive cruise control,

18
00:01:53,041 --> 00:01:58,600
which senses cars ahead and alters speeds accordingly, using a variety of different sensors.

19
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:07,000
Cars will communicate with each other, thereby avoiding collisions and eliminating faulty human judgment.

20
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:15,040
Driving with ITS will be faster and safer, allowing us to reduce congestion and better manage our existing roadways.

21
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:23,100
Traffic management is a relatively recent concept, less than a hundred years old.

22
00:02:23,101 --> 00:02:28,100
But our need to transport ourselves and our goods goes back to prehistoric times,

23
00:02:28,101 --> 00:02:33,100
when foot traffic simply followed the narrow tracks made by animals.

24
00:02:33,101 --> 00:02:37,100
But these old paths proved inadequate once we domesticated animals

25
00:02:37,101 --> 00:02:41,100
and began using them for dragging and hauling.

26
00:02:41,101 --> 00:02:46,820
The development of the wheel in 5000 BC meant that stronger manufactured roads were needed,

27
00:02:46,821 --> 00:02:54,580
so roads made of stone started appearing in the Middle East as early as 4000 BC.

28
00:02:54,581 --> 00:02:57,720
Trade was the major factor in the evolution of roads.

29
00:02:57,721 --> 00:03:02,660
The Etruscans began developing the Amber Roads, an extensive network of trade routes in Europe

30
00:03:02,661 --> 00:03:05,760
as early as 1900 BC.

31
00:03:05,761 --> 00:03:10,480
And trade also prompted the development of the famous Silk Road, a collection of caravan

32
00:03:10,481 --> 00:03:16,480
that eventually became an active trade route between China and the Mediterranean.

33
00:03:16,481 --> 00:03:22,480
War was as much a stimulus for road building and transport as trade.

34
00:03:22,481 --> 00:03:27,480
The Persian Royal Road, built by King Darius I in 500 BC,

35
00:03:27,481 --> 00:03:34,480
was the first example of a road constructed primarily for military or administrative reasons.

36
00:03:34,481 --> 00:03:37,480
In an early attempt at traffic control and efficiency,

37
00:03:37,481 --> 00:03:42,980
Darius ordered that his road should bypass many significant towns to reduce travel time

38
00:03:42,981 --> 00:03:49,480
and to avoid the main population centers where resistance to his troop movements would be the strongest.

39
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:55,480
Conquest also prompted the remarkable road building achievements of the Romans,

40
00:03:55,481 --> 00:03:59,980
who created a road system that served Europe, Asia Minor and Northern Africa

41
00:03:59,981 --> 00:04:03,480
until the coming of the train two millennia later.

42
00:04:03,481 --> 00:04:08,480
The Romans created the first traffic information devices with their milestones,

43
00:04:08,481 --> 00:04:13,480
which indicated the distance in leagues to Rome from any point in the Empire.

44
00:04:13,481 --> 00:04:20,480
And they were also the first to introduce one-way streets in an effort to minimize congestion in the city center.

45
00:04:20,481 --> 00:04:32,480
In ancient Rome, traffic congestion was so severe that at one point Julius Caesar banned carts from the central city to avoid the problems that they were causing.

46
00:04:32,481 --> 00:04:37,480
Traffic congestion was also a problem in 18th century London,

47
00:04:37,481 --> 00:04:42,480
so much so that the world's first three traffic cops were assigned to London Bridge.

48
00:04:42,481 --> 00:04:49,480
Selected for their unusual height, these three tall men were instructed to enforce new regulations

49
00:04:49,481 --> 00:04:55,480
requiring carts, coaches and other carriages coming into the city to keep along the west side of the bridge,

50
00:04:55,481 --> 00:05:00,480
while all traffic leaving the city stayed on the east side of the bridge.

51
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:09,620
In England in the early 1800s, the development of rail lines was prompted by the appearance of steam-powered vehicles on the highways.

52
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:17,660
When the gentry started objecting to the noisy coaches, the highly restrictive Locomotives on the Highways Act was passed,

53
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:26,760
which required that each vehicle have a man walking ahead of it, carrying a red flag in the daytime and a red lantern at night as a warning to oncoming traffic.

54
00:05:26,761 --> 00:05:34,760
The maximum speed of the steam coaches was limited to four miles an hour, so the vehicles took to the rails instead.

55
00:05:34,761 --> 00:05:42,760
One of the things you have to realize is that in a lot of transportation issues, there are not necessarily new ideas.

56
00:05:42,761 --> 00:05:46,760
There are reapplication of existing ideas.

57
00:05:46,761 --> 00:05:56,520
For instance, signals, modern signals, trace their roots back to railroad lamps to a large extent.

58
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:02,420
Those signals trace their origins back to fires, lighthouses,

59
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:08,580
things that were used for navigational purposes that may not have been part of a road or a highway,

60
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:10,680
but a lighthouse out on a peak.

61
00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:15,960
The railroads became increasingly important in both England and America.

62
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:24,620
and a firm of British railway signal manufacturers made the first lighted device for controlling street traffic.

63
00:06:24,621 --> 00:06:30,320
London's Houses of Parliament were the site of the first traffic signal using colored lights.

64
00:06:30,321 --> 00:06:38,860
Installed late in 1868, the 22-foot-high signal used manually activated semaphores with red and green gas lights at night

65
00:06:38,861 --> 00:06:43,480
to indicate stop or caution to all persons in charge of vehicles or horses.

66
00:06:46,340 --> 00:06:48,980
The new device was not entirely successful,

67
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:53,180
but the police commission recommended that the semaphore signal be modified

68
00:06:53,181 --> 00:06:55,120
and installed in other parts of the city.

69
00:06:56,200 --> 00:07:00,320
Meanwhile, other countries began experimenting with similar traffic control devices

70
00:07:00,321 --> 00:07:03,680
and creating some unusual signals of their own.

71
00:07:04,180 --> 00:07:08,340
New York City, all the big cities had congestion problems caused by horses,

72
00:07:08,341 --> 00:07:13,020
carriages, trolleys, and other common devices of the day.

73
00:07:14,020 --> 00:07:20,500
It became very obvious that there needed to be some means of controlling traffic at the intersections,

74
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:21,900
because that's where the problem is.

75
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:27,160
And one of the early pioneers in this area was William Phelps Eno,

76
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:33,860
who put a lot of effort into identifying the needs for traffic control.

77
00:07:33,861 --> 00:07:42,520
William Phelps Eno wrote the first printed traffic regulations of the New York police, issued in 1903.

78
00:07:43,520 --> 00:07:47,860
New York had actually had an organized force of traffic police since 1860,

79
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:50,680
but Eno recommended that more men be hired.

80
00:07:51,720 --> 00:08:00,020
By 1908, the Bureau of Street Traffic had 743 men, 138 of them on horseback and 18 on bicycles.

81
00:08:00,021 --> 00:08:05,320
and Eno added whistles to their repertoire of arm and hand signals and shouted instructions.

82
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:13,820
Even the most inspired efforts of the police, however, couldn't control the chaotic traffic of the early 1900s.

83
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:19,400
So in 1908, semaphores, or manually operated traffic control signals,

84
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:23,400
similar to the one which had first appeared in London half a century earlier,

85
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:25,940
started sprouting up all over the country.

86
00:08:25,941 --> 00:08:33,720
The semaphore arms, featuring stop and go in white on a green background, stood more than eight feet high

87
00:08:33,721 --> 00:08:38,620
and were operated by a traffic officer who blew a police whistle before changing the sign.

88
00:08:40,100 --> 00:08:46,460
Another popular variation on the semaphore was a beach umbrella with the words stop and go painted on the canvas.

89
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:50,380
The officer stayed underneath the umbrella and rotated it as needed.

90
00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:53,880
There were several problems with the semaphores.

91
00:08:53,881 --> 00:08:57,480
One of the most significant of which was visibility.

92
00:08:58,020 --> 00:09:06,420
And it can be very difficult for a driver of a vehicle to pick out a semaphore in the midst of a complicated urban scenery.

93
00:09:07,060 --> 00:09:12,860
Plus, it can be very difficult for the vehicles behind the first vehicle to see that semaphore.

94
00:09:13,260 --> 00:09:21,380
And if they can't see when they're supposed to stop, and there were no brake lights on vehicles in those days, then you start having collisions.

95
00:09:21,381 --> 00:09:31,380
The need for police officers to be in the middle of the roadway presented a safety concern to the police officers themselves.

96
00:09:31,381 --> 00:09:40,380
Introduced in Paris in 1912, the traffic tower offered police officers directing traffic both safety and visibility.

97
00:09:40,381 --> 00:09:41,081
visibility.

98
00:09:41,082 --> 00:09:45,980
But the anarchistic French drivers ignored the tower signals, and it was abandoned

99
00:09:45,981 --> 00:09:51,600
after only 22 days, but not before it had been observed by American traffic pioneer

100
00:09:51,601 --> 00:09:59,900
William Phelps Eno. Eno designed a crow's nest at the top of the tower and proposed

101
00:09:59,901 --> 00:10:06,320
this modified version for New York, but it was installed instead in Detroit in 1917 at

102
00:10:06,321 --> 00:10:11,000
the intersection of Woodward and Michigan Avenues, which was then one of the busiest corners

103
00:10:11,001 --> 00:10:15,600
in the world. That marked the beginning of a boom in American traffic towers.

104
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:21,334
Some of them were very ornamental. Some of them were very functional.

105
00:10:21,335 --> 00:10:22,580
Again, the traffic

106
00:10:22,581 --> 00:10:24,913
signals of the day had no uniformity.

107
00:10:24,914 --> 00:10:27,940
Some of them might be underneath the crow's nest

108
00:10:27,941 --> 00:10:30,548
that the police officer was positioned in.

109
00:10:30,549 --> 00:10:33,280
Some of them might be above the crow's nest.

110
00:10:33,281 --> 00:10:41,520
The concept was to put the police officer in the tower so he could see traffic on all four approaches to an intersection.

111
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:48,700
Even before the advent of traffic towers, alternating electromechanical traffic signals,

112
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:52,180
the true precursors to our own signal lights, began appearing,

113
00:10:52,760 --> 00:10:55,680
although their exact origin and location remain uncertain.

114
00:10:56,820 --> 00:11:00,060
Because a lot of this activity occurred separately,

115
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:05,020
Many people had similar ideas at different times around the same period.

116
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:10,000
It's very difficult to pin down who did the first white line, who came up with the yellow line,

117
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:12,600
who did the traffic light, who came up with the stop sign.

118
00:11:13,740 --> 00:11:18,380
Although there's many claimants, it's very difficult to say which was truly the first.

119
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:25,020
In the United States, the number of automobiles began increasing dramatically after the turn of the century.

120
00:11:25,021 --> 00:11:31,440
from about 8,000 vehicles in 1900 to more than 8 million in 1915.

121
00:11:32,460 --> 00:11:35,820
This meant that new roads would have to be designed and built

122
00:11:35,821 --> 00:11:38,820
and that traffic would have to be controlled.

123
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:45,700
The ancient Chinese deliberately introduced kinks into roads and bridges

124
00:11:45,701 --> 00:11:49,580
to keep them from being used by fast-flying evil spirits.

125
00:11:50,620 --> 00:11:52,860
Traffic will return on Modern Marvels.

126
00:11:52,861 --> 00:11:59,420
In the beginning, most signal lights were simply red or green until that proved inadequate.

127
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:11,280
Experience showed that drivers needed some advance notice that a traffic signal was changing from a green or go indication to a stop indication.

128
00:12:11,281 --> 00:12:15,820
Before deciding on the amber caution light we have today,

129
00:12:16,500 --> 00:12:21,380
traffic engineers experimented with several other ways of alerting drivers to a signal change,

130
00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:23,360
including bells and whistles.

131
00:12:23,880 --> 00:12:30,400
There are traffic signals that have countdown lights in the face of the signal.

132
00:12:30,520 --> 00:12:33,620
They have a big red ball and a big green ball,

133
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:38,640
and a series of smaller red balls and green balls in between the two big ones.

134
00:12:38,641 --> 00:12:41,120
The number of smaller lights would decrease.

135
00:12:41,280 --> 00:12:51,740
At the same time, state and federal officials also had to decide on uniformity in signals and signage, which differed tremendously from city to city.

136
00:12:52,900 --> 00:13:02,340
Finally, in 1930, the National Conference on Street and Highway Safety produced the Manual on Street Traffic Signs, Signals, and Markings.

137
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:06,680
Gradually, they settled on a set of signs that had certain characteristics.

138
00:13:06,681 --> 00:13:16,886
Shape was very important because in that era there were many drivers who were not literate and would not be able to read the message on the sign.

139
00:13:16,887 --> 00:13:21,320
Therefore a distinctive shape was chosen for each type of sign.

140
00:13:21,321 --> 00:13:26,880
And they decided that round would be the greatest danger, round having an infinite number of sides.

141
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:31,380
And they decided that that would apply to railroad crossings.

142
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:36,660
The next shape they decided would be an eight-sided shape.

143
00:13:37,020 --> 00:13:41,840
And that would be used at intersections to indicate the need for a stop.

144
00:13:42,540 --> 00:13:47,180
And then they used a four-sided sign to provide a warning.

145
00:13:47,181 --> 00:13:56,080
In 1932, the year that the first formal traffic engineering codes were established in the U.S.,

146
00:13:56,081 --> 00:14:02,780
Germany built the world's first freeway, the Autobahn, a 20-kilometer stretch running from Bonn to Cologne.

147
00:14:04,380 --> 00:14:10,700
Hitler saw the military advantage of having a four-lane nationwide system of roads with controlled access,

148
00:14:11,740 --> 00:14:15,160
roads where vehicles could enter and leave without interrupting traffic.

149
00:14:17,180 --> 00:14:19,346
In 1937, President Franklin D.

150
00:14:19,347 --> 00:14:26,060
Roosevelt proposed a national highway system for the United States, similar to the Autobahns.

151
00:14:26,660 --> 00:14:35,720
Although the hugely ambitious project wouldn't begin for nearly two decades, it was designed to solve congestion and other problems of the 30s and 40s.

152
00:14:35,721 --> 00:14:44,220
The cities were facing problems as a result of suburbanization, tax base moving to the

153
00:14:44,221 --> 00:14:52,120
suburbs, leaving behind blighted areas of low-income residents, creating drains on city

154
00:14:52,121 --> 00:14:57,160
services at the same time that the tax base is moving elsewhere.

155
00:14:57,161 --> 00:15:02,560
With these problems in mind, the people behind the interstate system pictured it as a way

156
00:15:02,561 --> 00:15:06,560
of saving the city. In the 1990s,

157
00:15:06,561 --> 00:15:11,640
we blamed the interstate system for causing problems that we were intending to

158
00:15:11,641 --> 00:15:15,640
solve in the first place, namely congestion, suburbanization,

159
00:15:15,641 --> 00:15:22,640
and sprawl. For a nation caught between the dual crises of the Great Depression

160
00:15:22,641 --> 00:15:23,360
and World War II,

161
00:15:23,361 --> 00:15:27,280
the 1939 World's Fair in New York offered hope

162
00:15:27,281 --> 00:15:32,160
and a promise of better times. Featuring the work of the world's foremost

163
00:15:32,161 --> 00:15:32,861
designers.

164
00:15:32,862 --> 00:15:37,760
The fair, called Building the World of Tomorrow, was the last grand showcase of

165
00:15:37,761 --> 00:15:39,024
the industrial age.

166
00:15:39,025 --> 00:15:43,880
And its most popular exhibit was Futurama at the General Motors Pavilion,

167
00:15:43,881 --> 00:15:46,620
designed by Norman Bel Geddes.

168
00:15:46,621 --> 00:15:54,980
Futurama gave visitors a tour of the magic motorways that were expected to be in existence

169
00:15:54,981 --> 00:15:55,681
by 1960.

170
00:15:55,682 --> 00:16:03,140
Visitors were shown the superhighways, multi-lane, varying speeds depending on the lane,

171
00:16:03,141 --> 00:16:10,160
and traffic was controlled by monitors in towers who sent signals to the vehicles

172
00:16:10,161 --> 00:16:13,580
so that they would speed or slow depending on circumstances.

173
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:21,264
Firebird 2 to control tower. We are about to take off on the highway of tomorrow.

174
00:16:21,265 --> 00:16:21,965
Stand by.

175
00:16:24,980 --> 00:16:33,260
Despite the optimism of Futurama, the highway of tomorrow had to wait for World War II.

176
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:41,600
But when World War II finally ended, there was a building boom in roads and highways, thanks largely to President Dwight Eisenhower.

177
00:16:42,140 --> 00:16:49,020
When he was in Germany after World War II, he observed the highway network they had, the system of autobonds,

178
00:16:49,440 --> 00:16:54,860
that allowed the German military to move quickly from one part of the country to another.

179
00:16:54,980 --> 00:17:04,980
And when he became president, he recognized the need to establish funding for a program of interstate and defense highways.

180
00:17:05,260 --> 00:17:14,620
What we now call interstate highways was technically and correctly known as the National System of Interstate and Defense Highways.

181
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:21,280
Eisenhower authorized construction of the U.S. interstate system in 1956,

182
00:17:21,281 --> 00:17:26,100
little realizing it would become the greatest public works project ever attempted.

183
00:17:27,100 --> 00:17:35,060
At the time, the expectation was that the 41,000-mile network could be completed within 13 years.

184
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:38,840
As a result, Congress authorized funding to cover that period.

185
00:17:40,340 --> 00:17:45,680
The need to modify the highways to address environmental and other concerns increased the cost.

186
00:17:45,681 --> 00:17:49,960
The cost of right-of-way in cities particularly was higher than expected.

187
00:17:49,961 --> 00:17:56,600
to complete the system, which ended up 42,800 miles, took a little bit over 40 years.

188
00:17:57,480 --> 00:18:00,760
The standards for the four-lane interstates were highly regulated,

189
00:18:01,260 --> 00:18:07,700
with 12-foot-wide lanes, 10-foot-wide shoulders, 14 feet of clearance under each bridge,

190
00:18:08,340 --> 00:18:11,120
and access controlled by on- and off-ramps.

191
00:18:11,960 --> 00:18:16,280
You can think about freeways as an advancement on the street system.

192
00:18:16,281 --> 00:18:21,020
A bigger, better street, no traffic signals should allow you to go faster.

193
00:18:21,540 --> 00:18:26,240
The way you achieve that is to put ramps in that connect the different levels.

194
00:18:26,740 --> 00:18:32,240
You can have a so-called cloverleaf ramp, a circular ramp that joins, goes from one level to another level.

195
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:39,520
The grade-separated, multi-level cloverleaf that is such a distinct feature of America's interstates

196
00:18:39,521 --> 00:18:44,220
was invented in 1906 by a Frenchman, Eugène Hernard,

197
00:18:44,221 --> 00:18:48,120
and first introduced into American roads in the 1920s.

198
00:18:49,180 --> 00:18:53,020
Theoretically, the cloverleaf design is the simplest way to connect two freeways

199
00:18:53,021 --> 00:18:56,900
because it allows nonstop full access between the two roads.

200
00:18:58,480 --> 00:19:04,080
The more complex exchanges used on freeways can combine several variations of cloverleafs

201
00:19:04,081 --> 00:19:07,320
and are often called spaghetti junctions or spaghetti bowls.

202
00:19:09,420 --> 00:19:13,500
Road design and traffic signs are only a part of the traffic engineer's job.

203
00:19:13,501 --> 00:19:19,880
Coming up, the human factor, safety features, and computerized traffic management.

204
00:19:20,900 --> 00:19:24,280
We're designed for travel at 70 miles per hour.

205
00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:29,340
Higher speeds met better traffic flow and greater hazards.

206
00:19:29,980 --> 00:19:34,000
So traffic engineers, many of them based at the Texas Traffic Institute,

207
00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:37,880
work to design a more forgiving environment for freeway drivers.

208
00:19:37,881 --> 00:19:45,680
Safety is a big part of what's been going on in the roadway environment for the last three or four decades at least.

209
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:52,140
The more forgiving environment that we've tried to create is a function of a lot of different treatments.

210
00:19:52,280 --> 00:19:55,940
There's a lot of different ways to stop a vehicle from hitting a concrete pillar.

211
00:19:55,941 --> 00:20:08,140
You put up guardrails that redirect vehicles away from hazards or back into safer areas as opposed to letting them hit a ditch or some sort of fixed object.

212
00:20:09,300 --> 00:20:14,180
The design also improved dramatically with wider clear zones.

213
00:20:14,181 --> 00:20:19,720
You can run off a road and not hit something solid now in most cases.

214
00:20:19,721 --> 00:20:25,560
where in a street or a higher level street, early freeway design,

215
00:20:25,960 --> 00:20:27,900
there were a lot of fixed objects next to the road.

216
00:20:28,920 --> 00:20:32,580
Breakaway signs and lights, softer concrete,

217
00:20:33,680 --> 00:20:38,140
and crash cushions have all contributed to a safer driving environment.

218
00:20:39,320 --> 00:20:43,360
The traffic engineers don't just consider the environment in which we drive.

219
00:20:44,040 --> 00:20:46,660
They also take into account the human factor,

220
00:20:46,661 --> 00:20:52,920
which is the field of science that attempts to optimize the interaction between people and their environment.

221
00:20:53,860 --> 00:21:02,000
In the transportation environment, what we're trying to do is find ways that we can help drivers drive more smoothly, more steadily.

222
00:21:02,540 --> 00:21:06,240
We're also trying to find ways to help drivers to be safer in the environment.

223
00:21:06,241 --> 00:21:11,980
A human factors person might look at the types of colors and the size of the text

224
00:21:11,981 --> 00:21:15,820
and the type of text that's presented on signs on the side of the road.

225
00:21:16,660 --> 00:21:20,240
If you're a driver, you can't afford to be looking at a sign for very long

226
00:21:20,241 --> 00:21:25,800
before you're in a ditch somewhere because you're not paying attention to the driving task.

227
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:34,100
Much of the human factors research is conducted in TTI's state-of-the-art driving simulator,

228
00:21:34,380 --> 00:21:39,640
which allows scientists to learn how people's perceptions can influence their driving.

229
00:21:41,300 --> 00:21:43,940
I've put a series of cars on the side of the road.

230
00:21:43,941 --> 00:21:49,920
As I'm driving down the road, I become accustomed to the rate that those cars are going past my vision.

231
00:21:50,720 --> 00:21:55,360
We'll see shortly here, it'll be like blink, blink, blink, blink.

232
00:21:55,760 --> 00:22:01,900
As I continue at the same speed, the distance between the cars will decrease.

233
00:22:02,260 --> 00:22:06,440
And that gives me a sense that I am starting to travel a little bit faster.

234
00:22:07,440 --> 00:22:12,640
As we see here, it's blink, another car blink, another car blink.

235
00:22:12,641 --> 00:22:19,120
and the distance starts to decrease between the cars and in turn I feel like I'm starting to go a

236
00:22:19,121 --> 00:22:20,172
little bit faster.

237
00:22:20,173 --> 00:22:24,380
In turn if I'm expected to maintain a particular speed limit and I think

238
00:22:24,381 --> 00:22:29,846
I'm going faster my response is to begin to slow down.

239
00:22:29,847 --> 00:22:34,300
Now we're not going to park cars on the edge

240
00:22:34,301 --> 00:22:40,760
of the road to alter drivers perception and to make them drive slower or faster but instead of

241
00:22:40,761 --> 00:22:47,760
using cars or previous experiments have used stripes on tunnel walls, big obnoxious stripes.

242
00:22:47,761 --> 00:22:54,760
If we were to use small shrubs parked on a planet at the side of the road or fence posts

243
00:22:54,761 --> 00:22:59,620
at particular intervals, we can give drivers a perception of how fast they're going and

244
00:22:59,621 --> 00:23:03,760
then we can change that perception in order to alter their behavior.

245
00:23:03,761 --> 00:23:09,760
The Texas Traffic Institute simulator can be used for driver training and to demonstrate

246
00:23:09,761 --> 00:23:13,260
potential road and sign designs before they're applied to the real world.

247
00:23:13,760 --> 00:23:17,260
This is particularly useful when it comes to geometric standards

248
00:23:17,261 --> 00:23:19,860
or the curves and grades that are used in a road.

249
00:23:20,460 --> 00:23:24,260
One of the things the engineers did in the early days of the interstate is

250
00:23:24,261 --> 00:23:28,460
they needed to put in a road from point A to point B, and engineers being engineers,

251
00:23:28,840 --> 00:23:31,200
they drew a straight line, and that's where the road was,

252
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:37,520
where the natural topography would allow them to put a road straight and true.

253
00:23:37,521 --> 00:23:45,000
They found out that the accident rates on those absolutely straight roadways were higher than they should have been.

254
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:53,620
And part of the reason is drivers were bored to the point of falling asleep or being hypnotized, road hypnosis.

255
00:23:54,200 --> 00:24:02,380
The driver has to be challenged a little bit, and so a few curves here and there are good because they keep the driver paying attention to the driving task.

256
00:24:02,381 --> 00:24:10,700
The Texas Traffic Institute also has a fully functional traffic management center, similar

257
00:24:10,701 --> 00:24:16,380
to those in Houston, San Antonio, Dallas and Washington, D.C.

258
00:24:16,381 --> 00:24:23,720
Los Angeles first opened its traffic operations center in 1971 for the Alaska Project, a study

259
00:24:23,721 --> 00:24:28,700
that monitored a 42-mile loop of the busy L.A. freeway system.

260
00:24:28,701 --> 00:24:33,560
The purpose of the study was to test state-of-the-art techniques for improving freeway operations.

261
00:24:34,740 --> 00:24:43,360
The basis for the LABSCAP project was loops in the pavement roughly every one-half mile.

262
00:24:44,140 --> 00:24:51,040
That was a non-technical relay closed in the field when vehicles passed over the loops.

263
00:24:51,041 --> 00:25:05,005
That information was sent back to a central computer every 20 or 30 seconds to do the data analysis for any of the traffic management techniques that we employed

264
00:25:05,006 --> 00:25:06,740
during that project.

265
00:25:07,840 --> 00:25:17,240
In 1998, Caltrans, which is the California Department of Transportation, replaced the operations center with a sleek new traffic management center.

266
00:25:17,241 --> 00:25:29,032
Operated in collaboration with the California Highway Patrol, the TMC manages and regulates traffic flow on some 1,100 miles of freeway and highway in Los Angeles and

267
00:25:29,033 --> 00:25:30,240
Ventura counties.

268
00:25:30,241 --> 00:25:42,084
How we receive our information from the embedded loops and from the ramp meter system is that we have a network of fiber optic lines which brings in the information

269
00:25:42,085 --> 00:25:43,240
into our center.

270
00:25:43,241 --> 00:25:43,941
center.

271
00:25:43,942 --> 00:25:49,680
We have approximately 250 miles of fiber optic communication system that is already

272
00:25:49,681 --> 00:25:53,640
embedded out there in the pavement, which allows us to communicate with all our infrastructure

273
00:25:53,641 --> 00:25:54,416
out there.

274
00:25:54,417 --> 00:26:00,240
The infrastructure that we have, the loops and the ramp meters, are sending

275
00:26:00,241 --> 00:26:03,384
in back-end data every 30 seconds to the center.

276
00:26:03,385 --> 00:26:06,200
So over the course of time, we start to see

277
00:26:06,201 --> 00:26:10,660
a pattern, whether congestion is building, what is the cause of the congestion, where

278
00:26:10,661 --> 00:26:15,180
all of a sudden there is a lot of vehicles that are not moving.

279
00:26:16,440 --> 00:26:21,620
The function of the traffic management center is to give as well as to receive information.

280
00:26:22,440 --> 00:26:26,120
So in addition to the almost 200 closed-circuit television cameras

281
00:26:26,121 --> 00:26:32,300
positioned on the area's freeways and an AM low-band frequency highway advisory radio,

282
00:26:33,180 --> 00:26:39,820
Caltrans has more than 120 message signs to inform drivers of accidents or poor road conditions.

283
00:26:40,660 --> 00:26:47,140
When we see something that's occurring on the system, maybe there's an accident or some detour that we need to establish,

284
00:26:47,560 --> 00:26:53,820
we give that information out to the motor so that they can make some real-time decisions to take alternate routes,

285
00:26:54,300 --> 00:26:58,500
maybe to use another freeway or to use a city street alternative route.

286
00:27:00,080 --> 00:27:04,400
The Traffic Management Center is also the headquarters of the Freeway Service Patrol,

287
00:27:04,560 --> 00:27:10,240
which has 150 vehicles available to provide free assistance to motorists in need.

288
00:27:10,660 --> 00:27:20,720
who can contact the California Highway Patrol through a network of more than 4,900 solar-powered freeway call boxes located throughout the two counties.

289
00:27:22,060 --> 00:27:26,540
The Traffic Management Center operates 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

290
00:27:27,420 --> 00:27:35,660
Traffic in Southern California, which is now so congested that peak traffic periods, what's known as the rush hour, last nearly eight hours a day.

291
00:27:35,661 --> 00:27:45,797
Since there is no room in which to build new roads or expand the interstate system, experts agree that the only way to cope with the increasing traffic is to

292
00:27:45,798 --> 00:27:48,380
aggressively manage the existing system.

293
00:27:50,600 --> 00:27:56,820
Traffic management centers owe a great deal to other forms of transportation, most notably aviation.

294
00:27:57,540 --> 00:28:03,180
Next, just as road vehicles require management and control, so does air and sea traffic.

295
00:28:03,181 --> 00:28:12,440
Americans lose $100 billion annually in wasted time and spent fuel while sitting in traffic.

296
00:28:13,080 --> 00:28:15,640
Traffic will return on Modern Marvels.

297
00:28:17,420 --> 00:28:22,120
Although air traffic is an even more recent phenomenon than surface or sea traffic,

298
00:28:22,840 --> 00:28:26,080
air traffic control has influenced all forms of transportation.

299
00:28:26,081 --> 00:28:36,500
In the early days of aviation, there were no navigational aids, no traffic control, and no means of communicating between air and ground.

300
00:28:37,700 --> 00:28:43,520
Pilots flew only in daylight, in fairly clear weather, operating on a sea and be seen basis.

301
00:28:44,520 --> 00:28:51,180
The aircraft of the 1930s were reasonably slow, so pilots could readily see and avoid other aircraft.

302
00:28:51,181 --> 00:28:59,180
An organized system of air traffic control wasn't necessary because the only real congestion occurred in the vicinity of local airports.

303
00:29:01,180 --> 00:29:04,180
These airports bore little resemblance to the airports of today.

304
00:29:05,180 --> 00:29:09,180
The pilots themselves decided when, how, and where they wanted to land.

305
00:29:10,180 --> 00:29:17,180
But it soon became apparent that some form of traffic control would have to be introduced around airports, or the results would be catastrophic.

306
00:29:17,181 --> 00:29:26,180
The first means of air traffic control occurred in 1931 in St. Louis, Missouri.

307
00:29:27,040 --> 00:29:31,260
And there was a controller, the very first controller, his name was Archie League,

308
00:29:31,740 --> 00:29:35,700
and he actually cleared aircraft to take off and land using semi-force legs.

309
00:29:36,360 --> 00:29:39,900
And that's the very first genesis of air traffic control.

310
00:29:40,820 --> 00:29:45,480
There was no way to give separate instructions to two planes landing simultaneously.

311
00:29:45,481 --> 00:29:54,480
So the semaphores were soon replaced by light guns, which allowed the controller to direct a narrow beam of high-intensity colored light to a specific aircraft.

312
00:29:56,480 --> 00:30:03,480
The light guns also meant that controllers could come in from the weather and operate instead from a glassed-in room on top of a hangar,

313
00:30:03,481 --> 00:30:07,480
the predecessor to today's sophisticated control towers.

314
00:30:07,481 --> 00:30:17,995
As we've progressed, certainly the first high-tech piece of equipment that was developed was the communication capability between the aircraft and the air traffic

315
00:30:17,996 --> 00:30:21,500
controller, or the radio operator in the early stages.

316
00:30:21,920 --> 00:30:27,820
By 1935, many pilots were flying under instrument flight rules instead of visual flight rules.

317
00:30:28,400 --> 00:30:32,840
So there was a critical need to establish separation of aircraft flying between airports.

318
00:30:32,841 --> 00:30:40,660
At the government's request, four of the major airlines established a number of en route air traffic control units,

319
00:30:41,060 --> 00:30:43,440
the first of which was located in Newark, New Jersey.

320
00:30:44,200 --> 00:30:48,280
A year later, management of these stations was taken over by the Department of Commerce.

321
00:30:49,460 --> 00:30:52,660
The tracking operations were definitely low tech.

322
00:30:53,400 --> 00:30:57,220
In fact, I have a couple pieces of plastic that we call shrimp boats.

323
00:30:57,820 --> 00:31:03,780
We actually used to have a flat display that was used by the air traffic controller.

324
00:31:04,420 --> 00:31:07,720
And in that flat display, you could see targets.

325
00:31:08,560 --> 00:31:12,840
And they wrote on these pieces of plastic the call sign of the aircraft,

326
00:31:13,300 --> 00:31:15,460
along with the altitude of the aircraft,

327
00:31:15,920 --> 00:31:20,560
placed the shrimp boat on top of the target and used that to track the aircraft.

328
00:31:21,420 --> 00:31:24,100
They also had the benefit of primitive radar.

329
00:31:24,101 --> 00:31:30,600
During the 1950s and 1960s, in the air traffic control environment, we used military-type

330
00:31:30,601 --> 00:31:34,100
radars that came out of the 40 eras.

331
00:31:34,101 --> 00:31:38,540
Congress created the Federal Aviation Agency late in 1958.

332
00:31:38,541 --> 00:31:42,660
This meant that a single central agency would finally be responsible for developing and

333
00:31:42,661 --> 00:31:49,020
maintaining a common civil and military system of air navigation and air traffic control.

334
00:31:49,021 --> 00:31:55,100
In today's environment, we have a multitude of navigational aids, communication aids,

335
00:31:55,101 --> 00:31:57,900
and radar systems throughout the United States.

336
00:31:57,901 --> 00:32:03,180
There are over 40,000 pieces of equipment deployed in the United States to assist the

337
00:32:03,181 --> 00:32:07,980
pilot in moving from one location to another.

338
00:32:07,981 --> 00:32:12,620
At the Air Traffic Control System Command Center in Virginia, the director and controller

339
00:32:12,621 --> 00:32:17,340
of traffic management for the entire country can monitor both the weather and the position

340
00:32:17,341 --> 00:32:20,100
of every aircraft in the air over the United States.

341
00:32:20,620 --> 00:32:25,820
This is a representation of all the aircraft that are airborne in the system currently.

342
00:32:26,420 --> 00:32:30,920
There are over 5,700 aircraft that are airborne being worked

343
00:32:30,921 --> 00:32:35,420
by the 20 domestic air route traffic control centers within the United States.

344
00:32:36,540 --> 00:32:40,760
The aircraft, or the dots on the screen, represent individual aircraft.

345
00:32:41,560 --> 00:32:46,080
For traffic management purposes, which is what we use this particular presentation for,

346
00:32:46,081 --> 00:32:52,020
It certainly is more than adequate in being able to adjust traffic flows

347
00:32:52,021 --> 00:32:55,700
and understand where there are congestion areas within the system

348
00:32:55,701 --> 00:32:58,440
from our east coast all the way to our west coast.

349
00:32:58,960 --> 00:33:01,200
We're looking at the screen two-dimensionally.

350
00:33:01,320 --> 00:33:04,220
We're looking at a flat screen presentation,

351
00:33:04,580 --> 00:33:07,600
and certainly there is a vertical component to that in altitude also.

352
00:33:08,240 --> 00:33:13,000
And I've seen as many as 6,400 aircraft airborne at a particular time on the screen.

353
00:33:13,001 --> 00:33:19,780
However, from a capacity standpoint, certainly the airspace would handle many, many more aircraft than that.

354
00:33:20,720 --> 00:33:28,860
The sophisticated technology used in tracking aircraft has also influenced one of the oldest forms of transportation, marine traffic.

355
00:33:30,820 --> 00:33:36,880
In the port of Los Angeles, Long Beach, one of the largest ports in the world and the busiest complex in the nation,

356
00:33:37,560 --> 00:33:40,680
technology is given a helping hand by the port pilots.

357
00:33:40,681 --> 00:33:47,680
Piloting and pilotage has been around for hundreds and hundreds of years.

358
00:33:47,681 --> 00:33:53,680
Probably originated the first time a vessel ventured out of its own home waters

359
00:33:53,681 --> 00:33:56,680
and went into a harbor they were not familiar with,

360
00:33:56,681 --> 00:34:01,680
and they engaged the services of a seaman that knows the harbor.

361
00:34:01,681 --> 00:34:13,940
In ancient times, probably 1500s or even before, pilots charted or drew their own charts of local waters,

362
00:34:14,600 --> 00:34:17,460
and that's how pilots gained their value.

363
00:34:18,220 --> 00:34:23,320
Countries would fight over those charts even to get trade routes and entries into different ports in the world.

364
00:34:24,140 --> 00:34:28,380
The pilots know the local weather, the shoals, currents, and tides.

365
00:34:28,940 --> 00:34:31,720
They know the port well enough to be able to navigate through fog.

366
00:34:32,240 --> 00:34:34,800
And they also know where the smaller private boats are,

367
00:34:35,040 --> 00:34:38,540
where the fishing boats congregate, and how the port is constantly changing.

368
00:34:39,100 --> 00:34:43,120
The port has changed dramatically in the past few years.

369
00:34:43,320 --> 00:34:45,840
It's getting deeper, it's getting narrower,

370
00:34:46,280 --> 00:34:47,720
the ship channels are getting narrower,

371
00:34:48,020 --> 00:34:50,800
and the ships are getting longer and wider.

372
00:34:51,780 --> 00:34:54,880
The huge ships and the changes brought on by construction

373
00:34:54,881 --> 00:34:58,300
are only some of the dangers facing the ports, the pilots,

374
00:34:58,540 --> 00:35:01,560
and the people who sail into Los Angeles' Long Beach Harbor.

375
00:35:02,580 --> 00:35:08,840
Working with the various sectors in the industry presents a wide range of challenges.

376
00:35:09,800 --> 00:35:17,340
For example, the deep draft and commercial industry here presents one set of risks.

377
00:35:17,341 --> 00:35:28,840
That set of risks is a grounding and an oil spill, a collision, an explosion or loss of life or damage to a marine terminal or a facility.

378
00:35:29,620 --> 00:35:32,640
The pilot boards a ship outside the breakwater.

379
00:35:34,120 --> 00:35:34,820
Hello, Captain.

380
00:35:34,900 --> 00:35:35,600
Hello, Captain.

381
00:35:36,320 --> 00:35:37,040
How was your trip?

382
00:35:37,300 --> 00:35:38,000
Excellent.

383
00:35:38,680 --> 00:35:41,680
And then guides the vessel both in and out of the port.

384
00:35:42,220 --> 00:35:43,520
Master, good morning to you, Captain.

385
00:35:43,521 --> 00:35:50,340
Could you come outside just a little bit, meet me between the sea buoy and the gate, put up one line, center lead transom, please?

386
00:35:51,440 --> 00:35:52,160
Roger, wait a minute.

387
00:35:54,260 --> 00:35:56,340
NYK Altair, this is traffic, good evening, sir.

388
00:35:57,040 --> 00:36:03,560
The port also has the Marine Exchange, a unique vessel tracking service similar to an airport control tower

389
00:36:03,561 --> 00:36:05,781
that works in conjunction with the U.S.

390
00:36:05,782 --> 00:36:09,140
Coast Guard to monitor all ship movements within the harbor

391
00:36:09,141 --> 00:36:13,820
and within a 25-mile area of responsibility outside the breakwater.

392
00:36:15,500 --> 00:36:18,420
These are the traffic separation schemes.

393
00:36:18,600 --> 00:36:23,060
These are found all over the world in and out of your larger ports.

394
00:36:23,700 --> 00:36:26,360
They are sort of like the interstate highway system.

395
00:36:26,780 --> 00:36:31,460
Here you have your outbound traffic lane, and here you have your inbound traffic lane.

396
00:36:31,461 --> 00:36:35,680
This pink zone is a separation zone that ships are supposed to keep out of that area.

397
00:36:35,681 --> 00:36:43,520
The shipping lanes or traffic separation schemes are important near ports where traffic congestion is the greatest.

398
00:36:44,980 --> 00:36:49,680
With regard to sea lanes on the open high seas, it's wide open.

399
00:36:50,080 --> 00:36:52,700
There are no designations other than weather.

400
00:36:53,140 --> 00:36:56,340
Normally when you go on Trans-Pacific, you don't have all that much traffic.

401
00:36:56,560 --> 00:37:03,700
When you start getting closer to the ports, that's when you start getting that traffic, much like on a highway system.

402
00:37:03,701 --> 00:37:09,540
Los Angeles, Long Beach is the world's third largest container port in terms of volume.

403
00:37:09,541 --> 00:37:13,420
KB260, APL Korea.

404
00:37:13,421 --> 00:37:16,420
APL Korea, KB260.

405
00:37:16,421 --> 00:37:21,220
All the containers that have come into Los Angeles also have to go out.

406
00:37:21,221 --> 00:37:27,940
So the harbor complex is a hive of traffic activity, rail and surface traffic as well as maritime.

407
00:37:27,941 --> 00:37:35,320
When the ship does come into port, it is a constant cycle of discharging and loading at the same time.

408
00:37:36,040 --> 00:37:40,080
Depending upon the commodity and depending upon the mode of transportation,

409
00:37:40,820 --> 00:37:45,880
some of it can be moved as quickly as three hours after it is discharged.

410
00:37:46,360 --> 00:37:51,220
Our on-dock rail environment, our goal is that once it is discharged from the vessel,

411
00:37:51,221 --> 00:37:57,120
within eight hours it will be reloaded and departing from this terminal for cities across North America.

412
00:37:57,940 --> 00:38:04,180
The goods that have arrived in the port on container ships are moved out on trucks and trains,

413
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:09,220
thereby becoming a large part of our surface transportation and traffic systems.

414
00:38:10,600 --> 00:38:12,980
Next, the future of surface traffic.

415
00:38:14,820 --> 00:38:19,000
Although engineers try to estimate future capacity when they build roads,

416
00:38:19,540 --> 00:38:24,140
there was no way of knowing what sort of traffic volume the interstate highway system would have to handle.

417
00:38:24,141 --> 00:38:38,080
Many of the roads that are out there were originally designed for traffic volumes that were projected to be 20 or 25 years in the future.

418
00:38:38,980 --> 00:38:45,080
They reached those traffic volumes in 10 years, and that was 30 years ago.

419
00:38:45,081 --> 00:38:56,280
So we need to be inventive in how we move more vehicles and move more people and more goods through our surface transportation system.

420
00:38:58,060 --> 00:39:04,420
One method of increasing volume on that system has been the high occupancy vehicle lanes, or HOVs.

421
00:39:04,880 --> 00:39:10,560
Although they have proven remarkably effective, they were met with a certain amount of resistance in many parts of the country.

422
00:39:11,540 --> 00:39:16,680
There's a perception that the HOV lanes aren't being used.

423
00:39:17,180 --> 00:39:23,600
Sometimes they're operating right where the HOV lanes are the most effective,

424
00:39:23,601 --> 00:39:28,440
which means they're still in free flow, and you're getting that tremendous time savings.

425
00:39:28,940 --> 00:39:35,260
But you're getting enough use that you're carrying double what the lane next to it is carrying.

426
00:39:37,040 --> 00:39:39,260
HOV lanes are only part of the solution.

427
00:39:40,060 --> 00:39:45,200
Today, the greatest hope for traffic management lies in intelligent transportation systems technology.

428
00:39:46,240 --> 00:39:53,920
One of the most comprehensive tests of ITS was conducted in 1997 on Interstate 15 near San Diego, California,

429
00:39:54,280 --> 00:39:57,480
where the carpool lane was used to test smart cars.

430
00:39:58,040 --> 00:40:04,960
Fully automated vehicles controlled by computers sense vehicles or obstacles ahead and alter speed accordingly.

431
00:40:04,961 --> 00:40:09,960
Vehicle-to-vehicle communication is also an important element of ITS.

432
00:40:09,961 --> 00:40:23,960
Essentially, one vehicle communicates to another that I am going to leave the automated lane and go into the manual lane.

433
00:40:23,961 --> 00:40:34,960
Well, the platoon of cars has to open up from its very tight configuration to allow that car to get out and then close up.

434
00:40:34,961 --> 00:40:42,960
Or the car will communicate, I want to come in from the manual lane to the automated lane.

435
00:40:42,961 --> 00:40:51,200
you are putting sensors on the front of the car to see what is ahead of it

436
00:40:51,201 --> 00:40:55,540
and to warn the driver that something is in front.

437
00:40:56,940 --> 00:41:02,640
The next stage of that is to actually have the automobile react

438
00:41:02,641 --> 00:41:06,400
because the machine reaction, this is hard for people to believe,

439
00:41:06,800 --> 00:41:12,060
but a machine reaction is much, much faster than a human reaction.

440
00:41:12,061 --> 00:41:23,240
And so we're looking at then being able to take better advantage of those few seconds of time to prevent the accident.

441
00:41:24,240 --> 00:41:30,280
Intelligent transportation systems present yet another challenge for scientists studying human factors.

442
00:41:31,240 --> 00:41:36,940
Before more advanced technologies are put into cars, we need to ask some serious questions about

443
00:41:36,941 --> 00:41:45,920
how are people going to react and how are people going to perceive the advent of technology in vehicles and on roadways.

444
00:41:46,260 --> 00:41:47,200
People are going to be scared.

445
00:41:47,201 --> 00:41:54,920
What happens when this car takes control away from me and it's controlling where I'm driving?

446
00:41:55,500 --> 00:41:59,580
Is it going to be as easy as my just picking up the newspaper and I punch a button

447
00:41:59,581 --> 00:42:03,840
and it starts to drive around the world and drives me off to work and to the grocery store?

448
00:42:04,740 --> 00:42:09,260
Japan and Europe have already introduced many ITS elements into their cars,

449
00:42:09,800 --> 00:42:14,500
including in-vehicle navigation systems combined with personalized traffic information.

450
00:42:15,100 --> 00:42:21,000
It is no longer telling you the most direct route simply based on a flat map.

451
00:42:21,780 --> 00:42:22,800
It is intelligent.

452
00:42:24,260 --> 00:42:28,240
Today, the best route may be around the beltway.

453
00:42:28,241 --> 00:42:33,020
Tomorrow, it may be taking the other side of the beltway or an arterial.

454
00:42:33,021 --> 00:42:43,960
Depending on the incidents, what's happening with construction, how the signals are being timed for events during the day,

455
00:42:44,560 --> 00:42:54,020
all of that can come in from the traffic management center to the vehicle, overlaid on the map,

456
00:42:54,140 --> 00:43:00,660
and you have just told your navigation unit, I want to go from here to there.

457
00:43:00,661 --> 00:43:09,620
On-board navigation systems and other elements of ITS are concepts that date back to the Futurama exhibit of 1939,

458
00:43:10,340 --> 00:43:16,140
long before the United States experienced the great construction boom that produced the interstate highway system.

459
00:43:17,260 --> 00:43:21,720
Although we may never again embark on that grand of public works project,

460
00:43:22,160 --> 00:43:25,660
we must continue to maintain our roads and retrofit them for ITS,

461
00:43:26,680 --> 00:43:29,800
a substantial challenge for the traffic engineers of tomorrow.

462
00:43:30,660 --> 00:43:36,340
But experts believe that we will be able to meet that challenge, particularly if we look

463
00:43:36,341 --> 00:43:38,840
to the aviation industry for inspiration.

464
00:43:38,841 --> 00:43:46,560
It's getting the same capability that we have with a radar system, that we have with the

465
00:43:46,561 --> 00:43:53,440
air traffic controller being able to communicate with the pilot, providing the driver with

466
00:43:53,441 --> 00:44:01,880
the same amount of information that the manager of the highway system has so that that person

467
00:44:01,881 --> 00:44:07,140
can make the most intelligent decision on which route I ought to take, what time I ought

468
00:44:07,141 --> 00:44:11,480
to travel, and what my expected travel time would be.

469
00:44:11,481 --> 00:44:15,800
A transcontinental surface trip in 24 hours?

470
00:44:15,801 --> 00:44:17,680
Not yet, but maybe never.

471
00:44:17,681 --> 00:44:25,960
But the traffic of the 21st century does promise to be safer and more efficient than the futurists of the 20th century could ever have imagined.

472
